freeb on OS-9 -- what was changed================================Bob Larson's `freeb', posted to comp.os.os9 on 22 May 1989: "Public domainversion: 5/21/89".  ORIG/ holds the posting's four files as they were sent.It lists the free blocks on an RBF disk -- how many, how big, and where --which OS-9's own `free' does not.Three changes, all under #ifdef OSK, so ORIG still builds where it camefrom.1.  `struct sect0boot' does not exist in this SDK's <rbf.h>.    Larson's headers had a structure of that name for the part of sector    zero holding the bootstrap LSN, the creation date and the volume name.    This SDK's <rbf.h> describes sector zero WHOLE, as `struct sector0',    with the same members -- dd_date[5] and dd_name[32].  A #define maps    one to the other.2.  The read starts at offset 0, not 0x14.    Larson's sect0boot BEGAN at 0x14, so the original seeks there.  Reading    this SDK's sector0 from 0x14 puts every field twenty bytes adrift: the    volume name came out empty and the date all zeroes, which looked like a    disk with no label rather than like a bug.  Sector zero of this disk    really does hold "OSK-Freeware" and a creation date; `dump' of the    image shows both.3.  The volume name and the year are printed properly.    OS-9 marks the end of a string in place by setting the HIGH BIT of its    last character, so `%s' on dd_name runs past the end into whatever    follows.  The name is copied out and the bit stripped.  And OS-9 keeps    the year as an offset from 1900, so the raw byte reads 126 for 2026;    the original printed it unadjusted with %02d.  It now prints the full    four-digit year.What was NOT changed: the free-block walk itself, which is the program.